Before you launch

Privacy policy – Since your form collects names and emails and you're in the UK, GDPR expects a short privacy notice saying what you collect and why. I can draft one in plain English.
A real domain and hosting – Right now the site only runs on your own computer. You'll want a domain (like setvion.ai or setvionsolutions.com) and a hosting service that can run Flask, such as PythonAnywhere, Render, or Railway. I can walk you through this when you're ready.
HTTPS (the padlock icon) – Most hosting services listed above give you this automatically, but it's worth confirming before launch, especially since you have a contact form.

Worth adding soon

Real content – Testimonials, past projects, or case studies (even one) build far more trust than the current generic service descriptions. If you don't have client work yet, a couple of "example projects" you've done for yourself count too.
Social links or LinkedIn – A footer link to LinkedIn or a company social page reassures visitors you're a real, findable business.
Basic SEO tags – A few extra lines in the page's <head> (Open Graph tags) control how your site looks when shared on WhatsApp, LinkedIn or Facebook — right now it would show no preview image.
Simple analytics – Something like Plausible or Google Analytics, so you can see how many people visit and whether the contact form gets used.

